4.28.m:
Come, you Ādityas, for wholeness;
become, O gods, bringers of welfare in the slaying of Vṛtra!
[…]

Narāśaṃsa, the prize-winning, seeking the prize,
Pūṣan who rules over men, we approach with favours;
[…]

O Bṛhaspati, make easy going for us always;
that welfare and relief which is established for mankind, that we ask for;
[…]

4.31.m:
Let Bhaga indeed be the blessed one, O gods;
through him may we be blessed!
To you as such, O Bhaga, every man calls again and again;
so be our forerunner here, O Bhaga!

4.32.m:
Come forward, be on our right;
then shall we two strike down many obstacles.
I pour for you the summit of sweet refreshment as a foundation;
let us both drink first in secret!

4.34.m:
In your ordinance the peoples settle down;
when you have risen, O bright-shining one, they stir.
You two and Savitṛ protect the creatures;
may the two of you release us from distress!

Extend your goodwill for help, O Savitṛ, O Vāyu;
exhilarate the mighty exhilarating [draught]!
Bring down the lovely [wealth] by the slope;
may the two of you release us from distress!

Wealth and prosperity may Savitṛ and Vāyu
impel for my body, skill, very kind;
place great freedom from disease in us;
may the two of you release us from distress!

Drive away bad deeds, [O Vāyu] and Savitṛ;
drive away yakṣma and the Śimidā far off!
For you two unite us with strength, with power;
may the two of you release us from distress!

4.39.m:
Whose are barren cows, bulls, and oxen,
for whom, the finder of the sun, the sacrificial posts are erected,
for whom the bright [Soma], adorned with sacred formulas, purifies itself:
may he release us from distress!

4.40.m:
Prosperously I drive off the calves;
prosperously I tie them to the tether-rope.
The first-fruits oblation belongs to the brahmans;
over that Kaśyapa has kept watch.

5.1.m:
Go away from our houses;
go away from the calves' tether-rope!
You will obstruct yourself here;
climb down from the kitchen!

5.3.m:
Up has flown that sun,
seen by many, slayer of the unseen;
rising, let him strike with his rays;
rising, he has made [them] sapless.

Down has sunk that sun,
seen by all, slayer of the unseen;
sinking, let him strike with his rays;
sinking, he has made [them] sapless.

5.4.m:
Let them sacrifice for me, whatever sacrifices are mine;
may the intention of my mind come true!
May I not fall into any sin whatsoever;
may the All-Gods protect me here!

May the gods win wealth for me by sacrifice;
may mine be the benediction, mine the invocation of the gods!
May the divine hotṛs win this for us;
may we be unharmed in our bodies, with good heroes!

5.5.m:
The earth is a barren cow; she bore Agni as embryo.
Protect this [man]! To you as such I would pay worship;
to you as such be homage, to you as such svāhā!

The year is a barren cow; she bore the full year as embryo.
Protect this man! May I worship you [the barren cow];
homage to you; svāhā to that [offering] of yours!

5.7.m:
The wind from all sides makes the mists look upon each other;
let the surges of the waters fly up separately!
Let the downpours of rain magnify the earth;
let all-formed plants be born separately!

Stir [the waters] up from the ocean, o Maruts;
let the glittering ones make the cloud fly up!
The bounteous ones cause it to rain forth;
let the plants unite with the saps of the waters!

Draw up the great cask, pour it out;
let it have lightning, let the wind blow!
Let them stretch the sacrifice widely released;
let the plants be full of joy!

5.8.m:
Sapless is your arrow-head, o arrow;
moreover, sapless is your poison;
and of a sapless tree
is your bow, utterly sapless.

Geldings are your diggers;
a gelding are you, o plant;
a gelding is that mountain, the ridge
from which this poison was born.

Let this water ward off,
brought from Varuṇāvat;
therein nectar has been poured;
that has made the poison sapless.

5.11.m:
May Agni assent to you;
may Varuṇa assent to you.
Then let a son be born to you;
armored, let him fight for cattle!

5.12.m:
The one full of sweetness placed the embryo;
god Bṛhaspati placed the embryo;
Indra and Agni [placed] your embryo;
may Dhātṛ place the embryo in you!

5.14.m:
You are the face of prosperity, the ray of truth;
with loud renown, go to heaven!
Rise up at the preparation of the oblation;
serve the gods well;
break away the residue!

Wide-striding you are,
commensurate with the breath of Vāyu;
strike away the scorners from me;
may I be released from distress!

Drive away the rakṣases with your fiery brilliance;
praise that oblation for the gods!
You are spacious […].

Fly high, eagle, up to that heaven;
making me dear to the gods,
hand me over to the ṛṣis!
Partake of the bright [soma] with the bright;
let the well-doers drink the sweet drink!

5.15.m:
Some eat the offered, others eat the unoffered;
both resort to the all-god oblation.
Unanimous, let them be exhilarated here;
intoxicate yourself with refreshment and strength for the sacrificer!

The presented first portion harms nothing at all;
make the soma-like honey according to wish!
Let those who eat what is not offered conduct the sacrifice well;
let increases of wealth attend the sacrificer!

Let the earths go down to your steps;
let the plants spring up, let refreshments speed forth!
O Maruts, strike the reservoir of Parjanya upon the ridge;
let good grain ripen, let the world rejoice!

5.16.m:
Upward we have made the boiled oblation;
mount the back, the domain of immortality!
O trees, spread out the sacrificial grass near;
with honey, with ghee all around you shall make it!

Whatever consumption is in the waters, that I appease for you;
with strength I anoint this cattle-pasture.
Breast-milk, milk I make poisonless for you;
sucking, go to the herd!

5.17.m:
The earth has held the ecstatic;
heaven guards the ecstatic.
For all beings, the ecstatic—
Indra held the ecstatic;
away I impel the demon from you!

Again the Apsarases have given you,
again the wind, again the directions,
again Yama, again Yama's messengers:
let them release you from distress,
for life, not for dying,
and for uninjured state!

5.18.m:
I have come to you with healing powers,
and with uninjured states;
your good ability I have brought;
away I impel what makes [you] sick!

These two winds blow
from the ocean, from the distance:
let the one blow ability to you;
let the other blow away whatever is defect!

This hand of mine is blessed;
this one of mine is more blessed;
this one of mine is all-healing;
this one touches auspiciously.

The waters indeed are healing;
the waters are chasers of disease;
the waters are healing for everything;
let them make medicine for you!

5.19.m:
Let the son be obedient to the father,
[of one mind] with the mother;
let the wife speak honeyed,
peaceful speech to the husband!

5.20.m:
This is the course of the water;
this is the breaking down of the wind;
this is the path of Agni's smoke;
not here is your course, O borer!

5.21.m:
Dyaus is our father, and Pṛthivī our mother,
and Agni, Jātavedas, who watches over men;
let them cast the takman downward, sinking low,
the ten-day one, far away from us!

O takman, these mountains
are snowy, with backs of soma;
they have made the wind our messenger, our physician;
perish away from here to the Maraṭas!

You who make fly up together
the balāsa, the cough, the udraja;
fearful are your missiles, O takman;
with them spare us!

He takes no delight in another's field,
the thousand-eyed immortal;
now takman has become well-disposed;
and he will be gracious to us.

5.22.m:
From whom the herbs go forth,
from whom all the trees do not fall away,
from whom birds move about in fear—
to that Rudra let us attend with an oblation;
let the evil-poisoned one depart far from us!

5.24.m:
Equal is the earth with the sun;
equal is the night with the day;
I make truth for help;
let the active [sorceresses] be sapless!

She made it, but could not prevail;
she broke her foot, her toe;
she has done good for us,
the portionless one for the blessed ones.

5.25.m:
You have been addressed around by the brāhmaṇa,
by Kaṇva, son of Nṛṣad;
like a flashing army you advance;
there is no fear there
where you reach, O herb!

Cleaving apart, hundred-branched;
'Cleaving Apart' is your father's name;
do you cleave him apart backward,
whoever assails us!

The non-existent arose from the earth;
that high word goes forth;
let that very thing, smoking away,
go back upon the maker!

5.27.m:
Nirṛti, eager to consume;
no father of hers is found, nor a mother.
From the middle […] all;
she did not attain [the realm] of the gods.

5.28.m:
Let Agni, knowing, receive this for us;
let Bṛhaspati, discerning, come to [it];
let Indra with the Maruts make it well-offered;
let it be free from consumption, free from disease for you!

5.30.m:
Here be the increase of plants,
and the gathering of the gods;
here indeed let there be [increase] of the Aśvins!

5.32.m:
All that King Varuṇa perceives,
what is between heaven and earth, what is beyond;
counted by him are the blinkings of people's eyes;
like a gambler his dice, he measures the worlds.

5.33.m:
You are invoked […];
you are the weapon of the seers;
foremost of the vow […],
become a hero-slayer, O girdle!

Let this vajra satisfy with the vow;
let it strike down his realm, his life;
let it cleave his shoulders, shatter his neck-tendons!

5.34.m:
Whistle, shake;
shake like the wind a piece of tuft!
With teeth […] in the two arms […]!
